A simplified shape of how a client integrates a remote edit while the user might be actively typing:

```javascript
function applyRemoteOp(localDoc, remoteOp, localCursor) {
  // Merge (via CRDT structure or OT transform) rather than overwrite
  const mergedDoc = mergeOperation(localDoc, remoteOp);

  // The user's cursor position must shift if the remote edit
  // inserted/removed content before it -- otherwise typing
  // continues at the wrong position after a remote edit lands
  const adjustedCursor = shiftCursorForOp(localCursor, remoteOp);

  render(mergedDoc, adjustedCursor);
}
```

The subtlety worth naming out loud: `shiftCursorForOp` isn't optional. If a remote insert lands three characters before your cursor while you're mid-keystroke, your next character needs to land at your *intended* position, not three characters off because the document shifted under you — a bug users experience as "my typing gets scrambled when someone else edits."
